<h1>tabulation hashing</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
The hash value is the <a href="xor.html"><em>xor</em></a> of values from tables (<a href="array.html"><em>arrays</em></a>) indexed by characters.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> h(c) = &oplus;<sup>n</sup><sub>i=1</sub> T<sub>i</sub>[c<sub>i</sub>], where &oplus; is the <a href="xor.html"><em>xor</em></a> operation, T<sub>i</sub> is the i<sup>th</sup> table, and c<sub>i</sub> is the i<sup>th</sup> character.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
hash function.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="twistedTabulationHashing.html"><em>twisted tabulation hashing</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="pearsonshash.html"><em>Pearson's hash</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zobrist_hashing" target="_blank"><em>Zobrist_hashing [Wikipedia]</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The typical hash function is the xor of the representation of characters, that is, h(c) = &oplus;<sup>n</sup><sub>i=1</sub> c<sub>i</sub>. </p> 
<p> The tables may be filled with random values or may have values chosen to avoid <a href="collision.html"><em>collisions</em></a> or for other properties. </p> 
<p> For games, e.g. chess, "characters" are pieces, like pawn, knight, and queen.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Albert L. Zobrist</strong>,  <em>A New Hashing Method With Application for Game Playing</em>, University of Wisconsin-Madison Department of Computer Sciences, TR88, 1970.
</p>

